@node Naming System
@section Naming System

In COBOL, user-defined words (variables, procedure names, etc.) use @code{case-insensitive}
naming (conventionally @code{UPPER-CASE} or @code{Mixed-Case} with hyphens).  All keywords
are case-insensitive but conventionally written in uppercase.

@table @asis
@item Keywords: ACCEPT, ADD, CALL, CANCEL, COMPUTE, DELETE, DISPLAY, DIVIDE,
  ENTRY, EVALUATE, GO, GOBACK, IF, INVOKE, MOVE, MULTIPLY, PERFORM, READ,
  SET, STOP, STRING, SUBTRACT, UNSTRING
@item Operators: +, -, *, /, **, =, >, <, >=, <=, /=, <> (or @code{NOT=})
@item Logical: AND, OR, NOT
@item String delimiters: Quotation marks (@code{"..."}) or apostrophes (@code{'...'})
  for non-numeric literals
@item Comment lines: Indicated by an asterisk (*) in column 7 or @code{*>} anywhere after column 7
@item Decimal indicator: S in PIC (e.g., @code{PIC S99}) counts as one nybble
@end table

@node Program Structure
@section Program Structure

A COBOL program consists of four divisions:

@enumerate
@item Identification Division — program name and metadata
@item Environment Division — configuration (typically fixed for Eightbol targets)
@item Data Division — variable declarations in Working-Storage Section
@item Procedure Division — executable statements
@end enumerate

@example
       IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.
       PROGRAM-ID. MYPROGRAM.

       ENVIRONMENT DIVISION.
       CONFIGURATION SECTION.
       SOURCE-COMPUTER. RICOH-RP2A03.
       OBJECT-COMPUTER. RICOH-RP2A03.

       DATA DIVISION.
       WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.
       01  COUNTER      PIC 9(3)    VALUE 0.
       01  SCORE        PIC 9(6)    VALUE 0.
       01  PLAYER-NAME  PIC X(20)   VALUE SPACES.

       PROCEDURE DIVISION.
       MAIN-PROGRAM.
           PERFORM INITIALIZE
           PERFORM MAIN-LOOP UNTIL GAME-OVER
           STOP RUN.

       INITIALIZE.
           MOVE 0 TO COUNTER
           MOVE 0 TO SCORE.

       MAIN-LOOP.
           ADD 1 TO COUNTER
           IF COUNTER > 100
               SET GAME-OVER TO TRUE
           END-IF.
@end example

For object-oriented programming, Eightbol supports CLASS definitions with FACTORY
and OBJECT sections containing METHOD definitions:

@example
       CLASS-ID. PLAYER-CLASS.
       INHERITS BASIC-OBJECT.

       FACTORY.
       METHOD-ID. CREATE.
           PROCEDURE DIVISION USING RETURNING OBJ AS OBJECT REFERENCE.
           SET OBJ TO SELF
           EXIT METHOD.
       END METHOD.

       OBJECT.
       METHOD-ID. UPDATE.
           PROCEDURE DIVISION.
           COMPUTE SELF/X = SELF/X + 1
           EXIT METHOD.
       END METHOD.
       END CLASS.
@end example

@node MOVE Statement
@section MOVE Statement

@subsection Purpose and Function

The MOVE statement transfers data from a source operand to one or more destination
operands.  It performs implicit type conversion between numeric and display
representations and handles elementary moves as well as group moves.

The MOVE statement is the primary mechanism for assignment in COBOL.  Unlike
COMPUTE, which evaluates arithmetic expressions, MOVE directly copies data
between identifiers, literals, or qualified names.

@subsection Syntax

@example
MOVE source TO destination-1 [, destination-2]...
@end example

@subsection Operands

@table @asis
@item source
@code{expression} — may be a literal, identifier, qualified name, or
subscripted identifier
@item destination
@code{identifier} — the receiving field; may be qualified (@code{SLOT OF OBJECT})
or subscripted (@code{ARRAY(I)})
@end table

@subsection Supported Forms

@example
       MOVE literal TO identifier
       MOVE identifier TO identifier
       MOVE identifier OF group-name TO identifier
       MOVE ARRAY(I) TO identifier
       MOVE identifier TO ARRAY(I)
       MOVE SELF/slot TO identifier
       MOVE literal TO SELF/slot
       MOVE NULL TO pointer-identifier
       MOVE ZERO TO identifier              ;; 78-level constant ZERO
@end example

@subsection Examples

@example
       *> Elementary moves
       MOVE 0 TO SCORE
       MOVE "PLAYER ONE" TO PLAYER-NAME
       MOVE 3.14159 TO PI-VALUE

       *> Move to qualified names (object slots)
       MOVE 100 TO X-COORD OF PLAYER
       MOVE 50 TO Y-COORD OF PLAYER
       MOVE 1 TO SPRITE-NUMBER OF PLAYER

       *> Move to subscripted array elements
       MOVE 999 TO HIGH-SCORES(1)
       MOVE PLAYER-NAME TO NAMES(INDEX)

       *> Move NULL to pointer
       MOVE NULL TO NEXT-PLAYER
@end example

@subsection AST Representation

The parser produces:

@example
(:move :from source :to destination)
@end example

@subsection Notes

@itemize
@item Moves to multiple destinations are expanded into separate MOVE statements
@item Subscripted moves require the index to be a positive integer or identifier
@item Qualified moves use the @code{OF} keyword (e.g., @code{X OF PLAYER})
@item MOVE with ZERO (78-level constant) emits the same code as MOVE literal 0
@end itemize

@node ADD and SUBTRACT Statements
@section ADD and SUBTRACT Statements

@subsection Purpose and Function

ADD and SUBTRACT perform integer arithmetic by adding to or subtracting from
an identifier.  These statements modify the operand in place (ADD TO, SUBTRACT FROM)
or produce a result in a separate identifier (ADD GIVING, SUBTRACT GIVING).

@subsection ADD Syntax

@example
ADD source TO destination
ADD source1 TO source2 GIVING result
@end example

@subsection ADD Examples

@example
       *> Add literal to identifier
       ADD 1 TO COUNTER
       ADD 10 TO SCORE

       *> Add identifier to identifier
       ADD BONUS TO SCORE

       *> Add and store in separate result (SCORE unchanged)
       ADD SCORE TO BONUS GIVING TOTAL-SCORE
@end example

@subsection SUBTRACT Syntax

@example
SUBTRACT source FROM destination
SUBTRACT source1 FROM source2 GIVING result
@end example

@subsection SUBTRACT Examples

@example
       *> Subtract literal from identifier
       SUBTRACT 1 FROM LIVES
       SUBTRACT 5 FROM HEALTH

       *> Subtract identifier from identifier
       SUBTRACT DAMAGE FROM HEALTH

       *> Subtract and store in separate result
       SUBTRACT DISCOUNT FROM PRICE GIVING FINAL-PRICE
@end example

@subsection AST Representation

@example
(:add :from source :to destination)           ;; ADD x TO y
(:add :from source :to source2 :giving result) ;; ADD x TO y GIVING z

(:subtract :subtrahend source :from destination)           ;; SUBTRACT x FROM y
(:subtract :subtrahend source1 :from source2 :giving result) ;; SUBTRACT x FROM y GIVING z
@end example

@subsection Notes

@itemize
@item ADD and SUBTRACT only support power-of-two operands in COMPUTE mode
@item GIVING form preserves the original operands
@item All arithmetic operates on the type determined by the destination PIC clause
@end itemize

@node COMPUTE Statement
@section COMPUTE Statement

@subsection Purpose and Function

COMPUTE evaluates an arithmetic expression and stores the result in an identifier.
It supports the full range of arithmetic operators with proper precedence.

@subsection Syntax

@example
COMPUTE identifier = expression
COMPUTE identifier EQUAL expression
COMPUTE expression GIVING identifier
@end example

@subsection Operators

The following operators are supported in expressions:

@table @asis
@item + Addition
@item - Subtraction
@item * or × Multiplication (power-of-two only)
@item / or ÷ Division (power-of-two only)
@item ** Exponentiation (constant integer powers)
@end table

@subsection Supported Operators in COMPUTE

@example
       COMPUTE TOTAL = BASE + TAX
       COMPUTE AREA = WIDTH * HEIGHT
       COMPUTE HALF = VALUE / 2
       COMPUTE DOUBLED = VALUE × 2
       COMPUTE QUARTERED = VALUE ÷ 4
       COMPUTE SCORE = SCORE + 100
       COMPUTE DISTANCE = (X2 - X1) ** 2 + (Y2 - Y1) ** 2
@end example

@subsection Bit-Shift Operators

For BINARY numbers, SHIFT-LEFT and SHIFT-RIGHT perform bit shifts:

@example
       COMPUTE VALUE = VALUE SHIFT-LEFT 2    ;; multiply by 4
       COMPUTE VALUE = VALUE SHIFT-RIGHT 1  ;; divide by 2
@end example

@subsection Bitwise Operations

@example
       COMPUTE FLAGS = FLAGS BIT-AND MASK
       COMPUTE FLAGS = FLAGS BIT-OR MASK
       COMPUTE FLAGS = FLAGS BIT-XOR KEY
       COMPUTE INVERTED = BIT-NOT FLAGS
@end example

@subsection AST Representation

@example
(:compute :target identifier :expression expression-plist)
@end example

@subsection Notes

@itemize
@item Multiplication and division are only supported for constant power-of-two operands
@item SHIFT-LEFT and SHIFT-RIGHT apply only to BINARY numbers
@item Expression evaluation follows standard precedence: parentheses, then */, then +-
@end itemize

@node IF/THEN/ELSE Statements
@section IF/THEN/ELSE Statements

@subsection Purpose and Function

IF executes statements conditionally based on a boolean condition.  The
THEN clause executes when the condition is true; the ELSE clause executes
when false.  Nested IF statements may be used for multi-way branching.

@subsection Syntax

@example
IF condition THEN statements [ELSE statements] END-IF
@end example

@subsection Condition Types

@table @asis
@item Relational: @code{=}, @code{>}, @code{<}, @code{>=}, @code{<=}, @code{/=}
@item IS EQUAL TO, IS GREATER THAN, IS LESS THAN, etc.
@item Class: IS NUMERIC, IS ALPHABETIC, IS NULL, IS NOT NULL
@item Sign: IS POSITIVE, IS NEGATIVE, IS ZERO
@end table

@subsection Simple Conditions

@example
       IF SCORE > 100 THEN
           MOVE "HIGH SCORE!" TO MESSAGE
       END-IF

       IF PLAYER-LIVES = 0 THEN
           SET GAME-OVER TO TRUE
       ELSE
           PERFORM RESPAWN-PLAYER
       END-IF
@end example

@subsection Combined Conditions

@example
       IF SCORE > 100 AND LIVES > 0 THEN
           ADD 1 TO LEVEL
       END-IF

       IF BUTTON = "A" OR BUTTON = "B" THEN
           PERFORM FIRE-WEAPON
       END-IF

       IF NOT GAME-PAUSED THEN
           PERFORM UPDATE-LOOP
       END-IF
@end example

@subsection Nested IF Statements

@example
       IF TEMPERATURE > HOT THEN
           MOVE "VERY HOT" TO STATUS
       ELSE IF TEMPERATURE > WARM THEN
           MOVE "COMFORTABLE" TO STATUS
       ELSE
           MOVE "COLD" TO STATUS
       END-IF
@end example

@subsection Complex Conditions with IS

@example
       IF HP IS NUMERIC AND HP IS NOT ZERO THEN
           PERFORM PROCESS-DAMAGE
       END-IF

       IF NAME IS ALPHABETIC THEN
           MOVE "VALID" TO VALIDATION-STATUS
       END-IF
@end example

@subsection AST Representation

@example
(:if :condition condition :then statements :else statements)
@end example

@subsection Notes

@itemize
@item ELSE is optional; omitting it creates an IF without ELSE
@item The NEXT SENTENCE form is supported but discouraged
@item Complex conditions may use parentheses for grouping
@end itemize

@node PERFORM and INVOKE Statements
@section PERFORM and INVOKE Statements

@subsection Purpose and Function

PERFORM executes a procedure (paragraph or section) and returns.
INVOKE calls a method on an object instance.  Together, these provide
procedure call and object-oriented method invocation.

@subsection PERFORM Syntax

@example
PERFORM procedure-name
PERFORM procedure-name THRU end-procedure
PERFORM procedure-name TIMES n
PERFORM procedure-name UNTIL condition
PERFORM procedure-name VARYING i FROM start BY step UNTIL condition
PERFORM inline-statements END-PERFORM
@end example

@subsection PERFORM Examples

@example
       *> Simple procedure call
       PERFORM INITIALIZE-GAME

       *> Execute procedure through range
       PERFORM PROCESS-LOOP THRU UPDATE-DISPLAY

       *> Execute N times
       PERFORM FLASH-SPRITE TIMES 5

       *> Execute until condition
       PERFORM READ-INPUT UNTIL INPUT-AVAILABLE

       *> Counted loop with varying
       PERFORM UPDATE-SPRITES VARYING I FROM 1 BY 1 UNTIL I > 10
@end example

@subsection Inline PERFORM

@example
       PERFORM
           ADD 1 TO COUNTER
           IF COUNTER > MAX THEN
               SET DONE TO TRUE
           END-IF
       END-PERFORM
@end example

@subsection INVOKE Syntax

@example
INVOKE object-expression METHOD "method-name"
INVOKE object-expression METHOD "method-name" USING expression
INVOKE object-expression METHOD "method-name" RETURNING identifier
INVOKE SUPER METHOD "method-name"
@end example

@subsection INVOKE Examples

@example
       *> Simple method call
       INVOKE PLAYER METHOD "UPDATE"

       *> Method with parameters
       INVOKE SPRITE METHOD "SET-POSITION" USING X Y

       *> Method returning value
       INVOKE PLAYER METHOD "GET-SCORE" RETURNING TOTAL

       *> Call superclass method
       INVOKE SUPER METHOD "INITIALIZE"
@end example

@subsection CALL ON Object

CALL may also invoke methods through the ON clause:

@example
       CALL "UPDATE" ON PLAYER
       CALL "DRAW" ON SPRITE USING 100 200
@end example

@subsection AST Representation

@example
(:perform :procedure name [:times n] [:until cond] [:varying ...])
(:invoke :object obj :method "Name" [:using expr] [:returning result])
@end example

@subsection Notes

@itemize
@item PERFORM creates an implicit call stack entry; GOBACK returns
@item VARYING creates a counting loop; FROM and BY specify start value and increment
@item INVOKE is the primary object method invocation mechanism
@item CALL ON uses string literals for method names
@end itemize

@node CALL and GOBACK Statements
@section CALL and GOBACK Statements

@subsection Purpose and Function

CALL transfers control to an external routine.  GOBACK returns from the current
procedure or method.  Together with PERFORM, these provide structured control flow.

@subsection CALL Syntax

@example
CALL "routine-name"
CALL "routine-name" RETURNING identifier
CALL "routine-name" USING expression
CALL "routine-name" USING expression RETURNING identifier
CALL routine-name IN LIBRARY
CALL routine-name IN BANK bank-id
CALL SERVICE service-name
@end example

@subsection CALL Examples

@example
       *> Local routine call
       CALL "INITIALIZE-GRAPHICS"
       CALL "DRAW-SPRITE" USING SPRITE-ID

       *> Library call (resident code)
       CALL "PRINTF" IN LIBRARY USING FORMAT-STRING

       *> Far bank call
       CALL "LOAD-LEVEL" IN BANK BANK-2 USING LEVEL-NUMBER

       *> Service dispatch (bank resolved at link time)
       CALL SERVICE "AUDIO-PLAY"

       *> Call with return value
       CALL "RANDOM" RETURNING RANDOM-VALUE
       CALL "CALCULATE" USING X RETURNING RESULT
@end example

@subsection GOBACK Syntax and Use

@example
GOBACK
@end example

GOBACK terminates execution of the current routine and returns control to
the caller.  In a main program, this is equivalent to program termination.

@example
       PROCEDURE DIVISION.
       MAIN.
           PERFORM SETUP
           IF READY THEN
               PERFORM GAME-LOOP
           END-IF
           GOBACK.

       SETUP.
           *> initialization code
           .
@end example

@subsection AST Representation

@example
(:call :target "name" [:bank bank-id] [:library t] [:using expr] [:returning var])
(:call-acc :target "name" [:bank bank-id] :using expr [:returning var])
(:call :bank :service :target "service-name")
(:goback)
@end example

@subsection Notes

@itemize
@item CALL "name" without modifiers is a local JSR
@item IN LIBRARY uses the resident library bank
@item IN BANK uses an explicit bank identifier
@item CALL SERVICE defers bank resolution to the linker
@end itemize

@node EXIT METHOD, EXIT PROGRAM, and STOP RUN
@section EXIT METHOD, EXIT PROGRAM, and STOP RUN

@subsection Purpose and Function

These statements control program flow at the routine level:
@itemize
@item EXIT METHOD — returns from a method
@item EXIT PROGRAM — returns from the program
@item EXIT — generic exit (returns from current scope)
@item STOP RUN — terminates program execution
@end itemize

@subsection Syntax

@example
EXIT METHOD.
EXIT PROGRAM.
EXIT.
STOP RUN.
@end example

@subsection EXIT METHOD

Use EXIT METHOD to return from an object method.  It emits the appropriate
return instruction for the target CPU.

@example
       METHOD-ID. GET-HEALTH.
           PROCEDURE DIVISION RETURNING HP AS INTEGER.
           MOVE SELF/HP TO HP
           EXIT METHOD.
       END METHOD.
@end example

@subsection EXIT PROGRAM

EXIT PROGRAM returns from the main program to the calling environment.
In Eightbol, this typically returns to the system/boot loader.

@example
       MAIN-PROGRAM.
           PERFORM CLEANUP
           EXIT PROGRAM.
@end example

@subsection STOP RUN

STOP RUN terminates the program unconditionally.  It should be the last
statement in a program.

@example
       MAIN-PROGRAM.
           PERFORM GAME-LOOP
           PERFORM SAVE-HIGH-SCORES
           STOP RUN.
@end example

@subsection EXIT (Generic)

EXIT provides a generic exit point.  In procedures, it returns to the caller.
In inline contexts, it may be used with PERFORM.

@example
       PERFORM
           READ INPUT
           IF INPUT = "QUIT" THEN
               EXIT
           END-IF
           PROCESS INPUT
       END-PERFORM
@end example

@subsection AST Representation

@example
(:exit-method)
(:exit-program)
(:exit)
(:stop-run)
@end example

@node STRING DELIMITED BY SIZE Statement
@section STRING DELIMITED BY SIZE Statement

@subsection Purpose and Function

STRING performs block transfer (BLT) operations, copying bytes from source
to destination with optional length specification.  This is the only STRING
form supported by Eightbol.

@subsection Syntax

@example
STRING source DELIMITED BY SIZE INTO destination
STRING source DELIMITED BY SIZE INTO destination LENGTH length-variable
@end example

@subsection Supported Features

@itemize
@item DELIMITED BY SIZE — copies entire source up to its PIC length
@item Reference modification — source or destination may use @code{name(start:length)} syntax
@item LENGTH clause — stores the number of bytes transferred
@end itemize

@subsection Examples

@example
       *> Simple block transfer
       STRING "HELLO" DELIMITED BY SIZE INTO MESSAGE

       *> Block transfer with length tracking
       STRING SOURCE-BUFFER DELIMITED BY SIZE
            INTO DEST-BUFFER
            LENGTH CHARS-COPIED

       *> Reference modification on source
       STRING INPUT-STRING(1:10) DELIMITED BY SIZE INTO OUTPUT

       *> Reference modification on destination
       STRING "DATA" DELIMITED BY SIZE INTO BUFFER(11:4)
@end example

@subsection Reference Modification

Reference modification allows selecting a substring:

@example
       name(start:length)
@end example

Where @code{start} is the 1-based offset and @code{length} is the byte count.

@subsection AST Representation

@example
(:string-blt :source source [:length length-var] :dest destination)
@end example

@subsection Notes

@itemize
@item Character delimiters (DELIMITED BY CHAR) are not supported
@item Multiple source operands with INTO are not supported
@end itemize

@node SET Statement
@section SET Statement

@subsection Purpose and Function

SET assigns values to identifiers, pointers, or index registers.  Eightbol
supports a subset of COBOL SET operations.

@subsection Syntax

@example
SET identifier TO expression
SET identifier TO NULL
SET identifier UP BY expression
SET identifier DOWN BY expression
SET identifier TO ADDRESS OF source
SET identifier TO SELF
@end example

@subsection SET TO expression

@example
       SET COUNTER TO 0
       SET FLAG TO TRUE
       SET INDEX TO 1
       SET SPEED TO MAX-SPEED
@end example

@subsection SET TO NULL

@example
       SET NEXT-ITEM TO NULL
       SET OBJECT-PTR TO NULL
@end example

@subsection SET UP BY / DOWN BY

@example
       SET I UP BY 1         ;; equivalent to ADD 1 TO I
       SET COUNTER UP BY 10
       SET I DOWN BY 1       ;; equivalent to SUBTRACT 1 FROM I
@end example

@subsection SET TO ADDRESS OF

@example
       SET POINTER TO ADDRESS OF DATA-AREA
       SET WORK-PTR TO ADDRESS OF BUFFER
@end example

@subsection SET TO SELF

@example
       SET OBJ TO SELF
@end example

@subsection AST Representation

@example
(:set :target identifier :value expression)       ;; SET x TO y
(:set :target identifier :value :null)            ;; SET x TO NULL
(:set :up-by identifier :by expression)           ;; SET x UP BY y
(:set :down-by identifier :by expression)         ;; SET x DOWN BY y
(:set :target identifier :address-of source)      ;; SET x TO ADDRESS OF y
(:set :to-self identifier)                        ;; SET x TO SELF
@end example

@subsection Notes

@itemize
@item SET UP BY and DOWN BY generate ADD/SUBTRACT respectively
@item SET TO ADDRESS OF requires the destination to be a pointer type
@item SET condition-name TO TRUE is not supported
@item SET identifier TO NULLS (plural) is not supported
@end itemize

@node GOTO Statement
@section GOTO Statement

@subsection Purpose and Function

GOTO transfers control unconditionally to a paragraph or section.
Eightbol supports simple GOTO and conditional GOTO DEPENDING ON.

@subsection Syntax

@example
GO TO paragraph-name
GO paragraph-name
GO TO paragraph-1, paragraph-2, ... DEPENDING ON expression
@end example

@subsection Simple GOTO

@example
       GO TO ERROR-HANDLER
       GO TO START-LOOP

       *> Label a paragraph
       MAIN-LOOP.
           PERFORM UPDATE
           IF GAME-OVER THEN
               GO TO END-PROGRAM
           END-IF
           .

       END-PROGRAM.
           STOP RUN.
@end example

@subsection GOTO DEPENDING ON

@example
       GO TO ZERO-HANDLER, ONE-HANDLER, TWO-HANDLER
           DEPENDING ON MENU-CHOICE

       GO TO HANDLE-A, HANDLE-B, HANDLE-C
           DEPENDING ON BUTTON-CODE
@end example

@subsection Paragraphs

Paragraphs are created by writing a paragraph name followed by a period:

@example
       PROCESS-DATA.
           ADD 1 TO COUNTER
           IF COUNTER > 100 THEN
               GO TO DONE
           END-IF
           .

       DONE.
           MOVE 0 TO COUNTER
           .
@end example

@subsection AST Representation

@example
(:goto :target "paragraph-name")
(:goto :targets ("p1" "p2" ...) :depending-on expression)
(:goto :target "p1" :depending-on expression)
@end example

@subsection Notes

@itemize
@item Use of GOTO is generally discouraged in favor of PERFORM
@item GOTO DEPENDING ON requires contiguous integer values starting from 1
@item Paragraph names must be unique within a section
@end itemize

@node INSPECT Statement
@section INSPECT Statement

@subsection Purpose and Function

INSPECT examines and transforms character data.  Eightbol supports three
forms: TALLYING (counting), CONVERTING (character substitution), and
REPLACING (pattern replacement).

@subsection INSPECT TALLYING

Counts occurrences of characters matching a pattern.

@subsection Syntax

@example
INSPECT identifier TALLYING counter FOR CHARACTERS
@end example

@subsection Examples

@example
       INSPECT INPUT-BUFFER TALLYING CHAR-COUNT FOR CHARACTERS
       INSPECT TEXT-STRING TALLYING SPACES FOR CHARACTERS
@end example

@subsection INSPECT CONVERTING

Replaces each occurrence of one character with another (one-for-one mapping).

@subsection Syntax

@example
INSPECT identifier CONVERTING source-string TO target-string
@end example

@subsection Examples

@example
       INSPECT UPPER-CONVERT FROM "ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ"
                         TO   "abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz"

       INSPECT ASCII-STR CONVERTING "AEIOU" TO "aeiou"
@end example

@subsection INSPECT REPLACING CHARACTERS

Replaces all characters in the field with a single character.

@subsection Syntax

@example
INSPECT identifier REPLACING CHARACTERS BY replacement-char
@end example

@subsection Examples

@example
       INSPECT PASSWORD REPLACING CHARACTERS BY "*"
       INSPECT BUFFER REPLACING CHARACTERS BY SPACE
@end example

@subsection AST Representation

@example
(:inspect :target identifier :tallying counter :for :characters)
(:inspect :target identifier :converting from :to to)
(:inspect :target identifier :replacing :characters :by replacement)
@end example

@subsection Notes

@itemize
@item TALLYING counts total characters (length of the field)
@item CONVERTING performs character-by-character substitution
@item REPLACING CHARACTERS fills the entire field with one character
@item TALLYING ... FOR ALL and REPLACING ... FOR ALL are not supported
@end itemize

@node EVALUATE Statement
@section EVALUATE Statement

@subsection Purpose and Function

EVALUATE provides multi-way conditional branching.  It evaluates one or more
subjects and matches them against WHEN clauses.

@subsection Syntax

@example
EVALUATE subject
    WHEN match-value-or-condition
        statements
    WHEN match-value-or-condition ALSO match-value-or-condition
        statements
    WHEN OTHER
        statements
END-EVALUATE
@end example

@subsection Simple EVALUATE

@example
       EVALUATE MENU-CHOICE
           WHEN 1
               PERFORM NEW-GAME
           WHEN 2
               PERFORM LOAD-GAME
           WHEN 3
               PERFORM SHOW-HELP
           WHEN OTHER
               MOVE "INVALID CHOICE" TO MESSAGE
       END-EVALUATE
@end example

@subsection EVALUATE with Conditions

@example
       EVALUATE TRUE
           WHEN SCORE > 1000
               MOVE "AMAZING!" TO RANK
           WHEN SCORE > 500
               MOVE "GOOD" TO RANK
           WHEN SCORE > 100
               MOVE "OK" TO RANK
           WHEN OTHER
               MOVE "BEGINNER" TO RANK
       END-EVALUATE
@end example

@subsection EVALUATE with Multiple Subjects

Multiple subjects use ALSO to connect them:

@example
       EVALUATE BUTTON ALSO DIRECTION
           WHEN "A" ALSO "UP"
               PERFORM JUMP
           WHEN "A" ALSO "DOWN"
               PERFORM CROUCH
           WHEN "B" ALSO ANY
               PERFORM SPECIAL-ATTACK
           WHEN OTHER
               PERFORM STAND
       END-EVALUATE
@end example

@subsection WHEN ANY

ANY matches any value for that subject:

@example
       EVALUATE INPUT
           WHEN ANY
               PERFORM PROCESS-ANY-INPUT
       END-EVALUATE
@end example

@subsection WHEN THRU (Range)

@example
       EVALUATE LEVEL
           WHEN 1 THRU 5
               MOVE "EASY" TO DIFFICULTY
           WHEN 6 THRU 10
               MOVE "MEDIUM" TO DIFFICULTY
           WHEN 11 THRU 20
               MOVE "HARD" TO DIFFICULTY
       END-EVALUATE
@end example

@subsection AST Representation

@example
(:evaluate :subject subject :when-clauses ((:when phrases statements) ...))
@end example

@subsection Notes

@itemize
@item WHEN clauses are evaluated top-to-bottom; first match wins
@item WHEN OTHER is optional; if omitted and no match, control falls through
@item ANY matches any value for that subject position
@item Multiple subjects must have same number of WHEN phrases per clause
@end itemize

@node LOG FAULT and DEBUG BREAK Statements
@section LOG FAULT and DEBUG BREAK Statements

@subsection LOG FAULT

LOG FAULT records a fault code for debugging or error handling.

@subsection Syntax

@example
LOG FAULT expression
@end example

@subsection Examples

@example
       LOG FAULT 1
       LOG FAULT ERROR-CODE
       LOG FAULT &HFFFF
@end example

@subsection DEBUG BREAK

DEBUG BREAK triggers a breakpoint for interactive debugging.

@subsection Syntax

@example
DEBUG BREAK expression
@end example

@subsection Examples

@example
       DEBUG BREAK 1
       DEBUG BREAK LINE-NUMBER
       DEBUG BREAK &HDEAD
@end example

@subsection AST Representation

@example
(:log-fault :code expression)
(:debug-break :code expression)
@end example

@subsection Notes

@itemize
@item LOG FAULT logs the fault code to a fault log
@item DEBUG BREAK halts execution if a debugger is attached
@item Both accept numeric literals or identifier expressions
@end itemize

@node Unsupported Commands
@section Unsupported Commands

The following COBOL commands are not supported by Eightbol.  Attempting to
use them will result in a compile-time error.

@subsection Arithmetic Commands

@table @asis
@item DIVIDE
Not supported except as part of COMPUTE with power-of-two divisors.
The following forms signal an error:
@example
       DIVIDE A BY B GIVING C
       DIVIDE A INTO B GIVING C
       DIVIDE A INTO B GIVING C REMAINDER R
@end example
Workaround: Use COMPUTE C = A / B (when divisor is power-of-two).

@item MULTIPLY
Not supported except as part of COMPUTE with power-of-two multipliers.
@example
       MULTIPLY A BY B
       MULTIPLY A BY B GIVING C
@end example
Workaround: Use COMPUTE B = A * N (when multiplier is power-of-two).
@end table

@subsection String Commands

@table @asis
@item STRING with Character Delimiter
@example
       STRING A DELIMITED BY "," INTO B
@end example
Supported form:
@example
       STRING A DELIMITED BY SIZE INTO B
@end example

@item UNSTRING
The UNSTRING command is not supported.
@example
       UNSTRING INPUT DELIMITED BY "," INTO A, B, C
@end example
Workaround: Implement string parsing using PERFORM loops and reference modification.
@end table

@subsection SET Variants

@table @asis
@item SET condition-name TO TRUE
@example
       SET GAME-OVER TO TRUE
@end example
Not supported.  Use MOVE or COMPUTE instead:
@example
       MOVE 1 TO GAME-OVER
       COMPUTE GAME-OVER = 1
@end example

@item SET ... TO NULLS (plural)
@example
       SET POINTER-1, POINTER-2 TO NULLS
@end example
Not supported.  Set each pointer individually:
@example
       SET POINTER-1 TO NULL
       SET POINTER-2 TO NULL
@end example
@end table

@subsection File I/O Commands

The following file operations are not supported:
@itemize
@item ACCEPT (screen input)
@item OPEN
@item CLOSE
@item READ
@item WRITE
@item REWRITE
@item START
@end itemize

@subsection Other Unsupported Commands

@table @asis
@item CANCEL
Cancels program units; not supported in Eightbol's execution model.

@item SEARCH / SEARCH ALL
Table search operations not implemented.  Use PERFORM loops instead.

@item SORT / MERGE
Sequential file sorting not supported.
@end table

@subsection Summary Table

@multitable @columnfractions .3 .4 .3
@headitem Command @tab Form @tab Alternative
@item DIVIDE @tab DIVIDE x BY y @tab COMPUTE x = x / power-of-two
@item MULTIPLY @tab MULTIPLY x BY y @tab COMPUTE x = x * power-of-two
@item STRING @tab DELIMITED BY char @tab DELIMITED BY SIZE
@item UNSTRING @tab UNSTRING source... @tab PERFORM with reference modification
@item SET @tab TO TRUE @tab MOVE 1 TO identifier
@item SET @tab TO NULLS @tab SET individually TO NULL
@item ACCEPT @tab ACCEPT identifier @tab Use input methods
@item OPEN/CLOSE @tab File operations @tab Not applicable
@item READ/WRITE @tab File operations @tab Not applicable
@item SEARCH @tab SEARCH table @tab PERFORM loop
@end multitable

@node Numeric Types
@section Numeric Types in Eightbol

Eightbol supports three numeric representations:

@table @asis
@item BINARY
Fixed-point binary with any PIC specification.  Bit-shifting operations
(SHIFT-LEFT, SHIFT-RIGHT) apply only to BINARY numbers.

@item DECIMAL (BCD)
Binary-coded decimal with any PIC specification.  The 'S' in PIC (e.g., PIC S99)
counts as one nybble, extending the range by one digit.

@item DISPLAY
Character representation (ASCII, EBCDIC, PETSCII, etc.).  Suitable for
screen codes but not for arithmetic.
@end table

@subsection PIC Clause Examples

@example
       01  BINARY-VALUE   PIC S9(4)    USAGE BINARY.
       01  BCD-VALUE      PIC 9(6)     USAGE DECIMAL.
       01  DISPLAY-VALUE  PIC X(10)    USAGE DISPLAY.
       01  SCREEN-CODE    PIC 9(2)     USAGE DISPLAY.
@end example

@subsection Decimal (BCD) with S Indicator

@example
       01  DECIMAL-WITH-S PIC S9(4)    USAGE DECIMAL.
       *> Supports -9999 to +9999 (S counts as one nybble)
@end example

@node Canonical AST Conformance
@section Canonical AST Conformance

The COBOL frontend conforms to the canonical AST specification defined in
@code{doc/chapters/frontend_conformance.texi}. Key conformance points:

@subsection AST Node Types

@table @asis
@item @code{:procedure}
Standalone paragraphs in the PROCEDURE DIVISION emit @code{:procedure} nodes
with @code{:name} keyword containing the paragraph name. Example:
@example
(:procedure :name "MainPara" :source-file "..." :source-line 40)
@end example

@item @code{:dd}
Data definitions in the DATA DIVISION use only @code{:dd} nodes (never
@code{:data-item}). The @code{:dd} structure maintains @code{:level},
@code{:label}, and @code{:attr} keys.

@item @code{:copy}
COPY statements are expanded inline at the lexer level before parsing. No
@code{:copy} AST nodes appear in the parsed output. If a copybook file cannot
be found, a compile-time error is signaled.

@item Standard Statement Nodes
All standard statement nodes (@code{:move}, @code{:if}, @code{:perform},
@code{:invoke}, @code{:call}, @code{:goback}, @code{:goto}, @code{:string-blt},
@code{:log-fault}, @code{:debug-break}, @code{:inspect}, @code{:evaluate})
are emitted in their canonical form as defined in the conformance spec.
@end table

@subsection Backend Compatibility

All backends (6502, 65c02, 65c816, HuC6280, RP2A03, cp1610, Z80, SM83, m68k,
i286, ARM7, F8, and stack) have been updated to correctly handle @code{:procedure}
nodes with @code{:name} keyword extraction for standalone paragraph compilation.

@bye
